<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Evaluate the definite Improper Integral and determine if it converges or divereges</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Evaluate the Integral below and determine if it converges or diverges.</p>
<p>Note: \( \infty\) is entered as <b>inf</b> and \( -\infty\) is entered as <b>minf</b>.</p>
<p>\( \displaystyle \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@func@}dx =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>
<p>Therefore, the integral is [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>First, we need to recall that we can’t do the integral as long as there is an infinity in one of the limits. Therefore, we’ll need to eliminate the infinity first as follows,
</p>
<p>\( \displaystyle \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@func@}dx= \lim_{t \to -\infty}\int_{t}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@func@}dx\)</p>
<p>We then carry out integration by parts to evaluate the integral</p>
<p>\(u={@let_u@} \qquad \rightarrow du={@du@}dx \)</p>
<p>\(v={@var_v@} \qquad \rightarrow dv={@dv@}dx \)</p>
<p>
    \(
    \begin{align}
    \displaystyle \int_{t}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@func@}dx &= \left[ ({@let_u@})({@var_v@})\right]_{t}^{{@upper_limit@}}- \displaystyle \int_{t}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@var_v*du@}dx \\
    &=\left[ ({@(let_u*var_v)-v_du@}) \right]_{t}^{@upper_limit@}\\
    &= {@expr3@}  
    \end{align}
    \)
</p>
<p>Therefore,</p>
<p>
    \(
    \begin{align}
    \displaystyle \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@func@}dx &= \lim_{t \to {@lower_limit@}}({@expr5@}) && \text{since}  \lim_{t \to {@lower_limit@}} e^{t}=0 \, \text{and} \,\lim_{t \to {@lower_limit@}} e^{-t}=\infty \\
       &= {@ta_1@} \\
    
    \end{align}
    \)
</p>
<p>Therefore, the integral is \(\text{@ta_2@}\) </p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
